<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 79 0 R/E(Not every job is posted on LinkedIn. In fact, a lot of companies only post a few roles there, even when they\220re hiring for much more.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

291 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 79 0 R/E(Why? Because LinkedIn is expensive.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

292 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 79 0 R/E(To post jobs on LinkedIn, companies often pay between three to seven thousand dollars per month. And in that package, they usually get only three job slots. So if they\220re hiring for seven roles, they\220ll only post the three most urgent or high-level ones. The rest? You\220ll only find them on their own website. That\220s why checking company pages is a must.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

293 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 79 0 R/E(When you build your list of 100 companies using ChatGPT or your own research, don\220t stop at LinkedIn. Go to their official website. Look for a menu item that says Careers or Join Us. Every serious company has one. And that\220s where they often list jobs they\220re not paying to advertise anywhere else.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

294 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 79 0 R/E(Companies also update those pages faster than LinkedIn. So you might find a fresh role on the website that hasn\220t even gone public yet.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[4]>>
